# The Phase 3 crushing map (design §3.1, §3.1.3).
#
# Two hazards, and they are deliberately different KINDS of hazard rather than
# the same one twice:
#
#   sector 1  a crusher. Its CEILING descends while its floor stays put, which
#             is the only way the gap a body occupies can ever close. A sector
#             that travels as a volume can never crush anything, however fast.
#
#   sector 2  a piston. Its footprint sweeps south through the room, shoving
#             anything in the way ahead of it until the far wall runs out. Its
#             floor is above step height, so a body cannot be where it is --
#             that, and not the fact that it moves, is what makes it solid.
#
# The piston is the half a client has to PREDICT: being shoved changes position,
# and position is what reconciliation compares. The crusher is the half it does
# not: the consequence of being crushed is a Lua decision (§3.1), and Lua is
# never predicted.

#   ^ y
#   |  +--------+
#   |  | piston |   sector 2, y 20..30, slides to y 1..11
#   |  +--------+
#   |  |  room  | crusher |   sectors 0 and 1
#   |  +--------+---------+
#   +-------------------------> x

sector floor 0 ceil 8 tag 1
  vertex  0   0   wall
  vertex 20   0   neighbor 1
  vertex 20  20   neighbor 2
  vertex  0  20   wall
end

# tag 200+N: a crusher whose ceiling drops N units. Ceiling 8 minus 7 leaves a
# gap of 1 -- half a standing body -- so anything in here is crushed at the
# bottom of the stroke and released on the way back up.
sector floor 0 ceil 8 tag 207
  vertex 20   0   wall
  vertex 40   0   wall
  vertex 40  20   wall
  vertex 20  20   neighbor 0
end

# tag 300+N: a footprint that slides N units south. Floor 4 puts it well above
# STEP_HEIGHT, so it is solid to a body standing on the room floor.
#
# 20, not 19: a stroke that ends exactly one radius from the far wall pushes a
# body right up to it and stops, which is a shove and not a pin. The room has to
# actually run out for §3.1.3 to have anything to report.
sector floor 4 ceil 8 tag 320
  vertex  0  20   neighbor 0
  vertex 20  20   wall
  vertex 20  30   wall
  vertex  0  30   wall
end

spawn start  10 10 0
spawn trap   30 10 0

# Rails (design §3.4). Waypoints are SECTOR-RELATIVE: mapc infers the anchor
# from the sector each point falls in and writes it into the file, so a rail
# bolted to something that moves simply moves, and nothing that reads a rail has
# a special case for it.
#
# Two rails in the same map, differing only in what they are attached to. The
# wire rides the piston; the floor rail does not move at all. That contrast is
# the point -- one of them proves nothing without the other.

wire tag 1
  waypoint  2 22 6
  waypoint 18 22 6
end

rail tag 2
  waypoint  2  2 1
  waypoint 18  2 1
  waypoint 18  8 1
end

# A branch off the floor rail's last waypoint. Choosing between branches is
# Lua's decision (§7.4); the engine reports the fork and takes neither.
rail tag 3
  waypoint 18  8 1
  waypoint 18 16 1
end

junction 1 2 to 2 0
